course: world
lesson: 34
title: The brain, nerves and the five senses
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The brain is the control centre of the body. It sits inside the skull and is joined to the spinal cord, which runs down inside the backbone.
explain: Nerves are like wires that carry tiny electrical messages: sense nerves bring news from the body to the brain, and other nerves carry orders from the brain to the muscles.
explain: We learn about the world through five senses: sight (eyes), hearing (ears), smell (nose), taste (tongue) and touch (skin).
explain: Taste buds for sweet, salty, sour, bitter and savoury (umami) are found all over the tongue. The old "tongue map", with each taste on its own patch, is a myth.
explain: Much of what we call flavour really comes from smell, which is why food seems tasteless when your nose is blocked by a cold.
explain: Some quick actions skip the thinking part of the brain: if you touch a hot pan, the spinal cord pulls your hand away before you even feel the pain. This is a reflex.
fact: The brain controls the body and is protected by the skull.
fact: The spinal cord runs inside the backbone and joins the brain to the nerves of the body.
fact: Nerves carry messages as tiny electrical signals.
fact: The five senses are sight, hearing, smell, taste and touch.
fact: The tongue can sense sweet, salty, sour, bitter and savoury (umami).
fact: Every taste can be sensed by taste buds all over the tongue; the tongue map is a myth.
fact: Most of the flavour of food comes from smell.
fact: The skin feels touch, pressure, heat, cold and pain.
fact: The eye sees light that enters through the pupil; the lens focuses it at the back of the eye.
fact: The ear catches sound vibrations; the eardrum shakes when sound reaches it.
fact: A reflex is a quick, automatic action, such as pulling your hand off something hot.
exercise: Which organ is the control centre of the body? | The brain | The heart | The stomach | The lungs
exercise: What carries messages between the brain and the body? | Nerves | Arteries | Tendons | Bones
exercise: Where is the spinal cord? | Inside the backbone | Inside the skull only | Inside the stomach | Inside the ribs
exercise: How do nerves send messages? | As tiny electrical signals | As drops of blood squeezed along | As little puffs of air inside them | As sounds we can hear if we listen
exercise: Which organ is used for the sense of smell? | The nose | The ear | The skin | The tongue
exercise: Which list gives the five senses? | Sight, hearing, smell, taste, touch | Sight, hearing, thinking, taste, touch | Breathing, hearing, smell, taste, touch | Sight, hearing, smell, walking, touch
exercise: Where on the tongue can sweet be tasted? | All over the tongue | Only on the tip | Only at the back | Only on the sides
exercise: Why does food taste of very little when you have a blocked nose? | Much of flavour comes from smell | The tongue stops working during a cold | Germs eat the taste buds | The nose sends taste to the stomach
exercise: Which of these is a taste the tongue can sense? | Sour | Hot | Loud | Bright
exercise: What does the skin sense? | Touch, heat, cold and pain | Light and colour, like the eyes | Sound and music | Smells and tastes
exercise: What part of the ear shakes when sound reaches it? | The eardrum | The pupil | The lens | The taste bud
exercise: Through which opening does light enter the eye? | The pupil | The eardrum | The nostril | The lens cap
exercise: What is a reflex? | A quick automatic action without thinking | A slow careful decision made after thinking | A kind of dream | A bone in the knee
exercise: How does a reflex save your hand from a hot pan? | The spinal cord pulls it back before you feel pain | The skin cools itself down at once | The brain thinks about it for a long time | The blood carries the heat away
exercise: Why is the brain inside the skull? | The hard skull protects the soft brain | The skull is where thoughts are made and kept | The skull keeps the brain cold on hot days | The skull pumps fresh blood up to the brain
test: A friend says "bitter is tasted only at the back of the tongue". What is right? | Bitter, like every taste, can be sensed all over the tongue | Yes, the back is the only place for bitter | Bitter is tasted only on the tip | Bitter is tasted only by the nose
test: You hold your nose and eat a slice of apple and a slice of raw potato. Why is it hard to tell them apart? | Without smell, much of their flavour is lost | The tongue cannot sense anything when the nose is closed | Apple and potato are made of the same thing | Holding the nose stops the teeth chewing
test: If the nerve from an eye to the brain were cut, what would happen? | That eye could not send pictures to the brain | The eye would see better | The eye would start to hear | Nothing at all, because eyes can see without the brain
test: Which is true about nerves? | They carry messages to and from the brain | They carry blood up to the brain and back again | They only carry messages from the brain to the body | They are hollow tubes full of air, like straws
test: A doctor taps just below your knee and your leg kicks up by itself. What is this? | A reflex | A thought | A dream | A habit learned at school
test: Which sense tells you that the bath water is too hot? | Touch, through the skin | Sight, through the eyes | Hearing, through the ears | Smell, through the nose
test: Why do bicycle helmets matter so much? | They help protect the brain, which controls the whole body | They keep the ears and the hair warm in the wind on cold winter rides | They make you ride faster | They improve your sense of smell
test: A blindfolded girl knows her mum is baking a cake. Which sense tells her? | Smell | Sight | Taste only | Touch
test: How does the brain learn that a mosquito bit your toe? | A nerve sends a signal up the spinal cord to the brain | The blood carries the pain to the brain | The bone of the toe tells the skull | The skin shouts to the ears
test: why'd my hand jump off the hot pan before i even thought | It was a reflex, a quick automatic action | The hand muscles think for themselves | The pan pushed the hand away | The skin melted for a moment
test: got a cold n food tastes of nothing, how come | Most flavour comes from smell, and a blocked nose cannot smell | The cold kills the taste buds for good | The tongue stops working for the whole cold every time you sneeze | Germs eat the taste of the food
test: teacher said sweet is only tasted at the tip of the tongue. true? | No, taste buds all over the tongue sense every taste | Yes, the tip is for sweet and the back for bitter | Yes, the rest of the tongue tastes nothing | No, sweet is tasted only at the back
test: Jack says nerves carry messages like little tubes of water. right? | No, they carry tiny electrical signals | Yes, nerves are little tubes full of water | Yes, the water pushes the message along inside | No, nerves carry messages as tiny sounds
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the transmission of messages along the nerves? | They are conveyed as minute electrical signals | They are conveyed by the flow of blood | They are conveyed as sound | They are conveyed by the movement of air
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the sense of taste. | Every taste may be detected by taste buds across the whole tongue | Each taste is detected solely in its own region of the tongue | Sweetness is detected solely at the tip of the tongue | Bitterness is detected solely at the back and the sides of the tongue
exam: Identify the sense from which the greater part of the flavour of food derives. | Smell | Taste | Touch | Sight
exam: Which of the following is designated by the term reflex? | A rapid, automatic action such as withdrawing the hand from heat | A deliberate decision made after reflection | A memory of a past event | A dream occurring during sleep
